---
// The prose on this page is placeholder copy — rewrite it as your own. It is
// intentionally *not* in `src/i18n/`: the dictionary holds UI chrome, so a
// translator isn't handed someone else's biography, and you don't have to open
// a dictionary file to edit your own about page.
import { ORGANISERS, SITE } from '../../consts';
import { t } from '../../i18n';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={t('about.title')}
  description={`About the author behind this ${SITE.title} site.`}
>
  <section class="page-head section">
    <p class="eyebrow">{t('about.eyebrow')}</p>
    <h1>About the conference.</h1>
    <p class="lead"></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Profile</p>
      <h2>The conference, itself.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        RSC2027 will mark the 50th edition of the Research Students' Conference. This is a milestone
        made possible by the longest student-led conference in the UK. We are a student-run
        conference organised for students and early-career mathematicians that moves from place to
        place each year. Our goal is to create a rich, collaborative and diverse environment that
        brings together early-career researchers: proving an opportunity to network, explore open
        problems within the field, and develop as a mathematicians and satisticians.
      </p>
      <!-- <p>
        The Research Students' Conference in Probability and Statistics is the longest running
        student-led conference in the UK. Now in its 49th edition, this is a conference organised
        for PhD students by PhD students. The RSC creates a space for PhD students and pre-doctoral
        resarchers across the UK and Europe to come together and discuss their latest research in a
        collaborative, supportive environment of peers.
      </p> -->
      <p>
        For this 50th annivesrary, the conference will be held in London with a call for students
        across the UK and Europe to consider attending. Our committee is made up of fellow PhD
        students who are excited to share our work and to learn from yours. Each year, the
        conference has been held in different cities across the UK, passing from university to
        university. Last year (2026) the 49th edition was hosted in Cambridge. Previously, the RSC
        was held in Glasgow (2025), Exeter (2024) and Sheffield (2024).
      </p>
